# Files whose BYTES are load-bearing.
#
# This is deliberately not a line-ending policy for the repository. Everything
# here is LF in the index and LF in the working tree on macOS and Linux, where
# git's default leaves it alone, and nothing below is trying to change that. It
# is a list of files that a *checkout* must not rewrite.
#
# A Windows runner clones with `core.autocrlf=true`, so every file git
# classifies as text gets LF -> CRLF on the way to disk. For anything covered by
# a signature that is fatal and silent: the bytes that arrive are not the bytes
# that were signed, and the only symptom is a verifier saying the signature is
# invalid — which reads like a bug in the verifier.
#
# Measured, not theorised (#308, PR #343). The `Windows workspace tests` job
# failed on exactly one test — `a_signature_over_the_payload_verifies_under_its_own_key`
# — while every negative test passed, which is the signature of "the verifier is
# right and the bytes moved". Reproduced locally with
# `git -c core.autocrlf=true checkout-index`: `payload.tar.gz` arrived 72 bytes
# instead of 71.

# The updater fixtures (`crates/updatefeed`), byte for byte on every platform.
# All three, not just the one that broke: the payload is the signed data, the
# .sig is the signature over it, and test-key.pub is the key both are checked
# against. Today only the payload is at risk — the other two are single-line
# with no trailing newline, so there is no LF for git to convert — but that is
# an accident of how `cargo tauri signer` writes them, not a property anyone
# should have to re-derive when a fixture is regenerated.
tests/fixtures/updater/** -text

# The same hazard wherever it turns up next. `-text` rather than `binary`:
# these are base64 documents and a diff of one is still worth reading, so it is
# the conversion that has to stop, not the diff.
*.sig -text
*.pub -text
